            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Scene: The Argument at the Weekly Meeting</h2>
<p>Last month, a weekly meeting at a mid-core game publisher's office grew heated. Zhou, the head of performance marketing, slammed the table: "LTV dropped 15% this month, and it's because of those three new test accounts!" Liu, a campaign optimizer, defended herself: "I wanted to focus on those high-performing accounts, but managing 12 accounts in rotation, there's simply not enough time."</p>
<p>This company spends over $500,000 monthly across Facebook and TikTok with 12 ad accounts. They should have been a mature team, but in reality:</p>
<ul>
<li>3 main accounts contributed 70% of spend, yet used the same daily report template as 9 smaller accounts</li>
<li>One test account frequently triggered reviews, nearly jeopardizing other accounts under the same business manager</li>
<li>All 5 team members were constantly firefighting with no time left for strategic optimization</li>
</ul>
<p>What went wrong? Not budget, not creative quality—the issue was <strong>lack of account tiering</strong>.</p>
<h2>Diagnosis: Is Your Account Management Tiered?</h2>
<p>Many teams approach multi-account management with "equal effort": check every account once daily, respond at the same speed. But accounts differ dramatically:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Daily Spend</th>
<th>Lifecycle</th>
<th>Priority</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>High-value accounts</td>
<td>&gt;$10,000</td>
<td>Long-term stable</td>
<td>Highest</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Test accounts</td>
<td>$500-3,000</td>
<td>Fast iteration</td>
<td>Medium</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High-risk accounts</td>
<td>Variable</td>
<td>随时可能封号</td>
<td>Lowest</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Three criteria for tiering:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Spend share</strong>: Accounts contributing over 10% of total spend go to main tier</li>
<li><strong>Lifecycle stage</strong>: Accounts running 30+ days stably are main; under 7 days are test tier</li>
<li><strong>Account health</strong>: Accounts with consistently low Quality Ranking or policy warnings enter observation tier</li>
</ol>
<h2>Actions: Three Steps to Build Account Tiering</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Tag Every Account</h3>
<p>Don't rely on memory—use tools. Label each account with three tag types:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Main/Auxiliary/Test</strong>: By spend scale</li>
<li><strong>High-performing/Observation/Problem</strong>: By account health</li>
<li><strong>Main Product/New Product Test/Inventory Clearance</strong>: By campaign objective</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, "Main-High-Performing-Main Product" gets top priority maintenance, while "Test-Observation-New Product Test" only needs weekly review.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Configure Different Inspection Frequencies</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Account Tier</th>
<th>Daily Checks</th>
<th>Response Time</th>
<th>Owner</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Main accounts</td>
<td>Every 2 hours</td>
<td>Within 15 minutes</td>
<td>Senior optimizer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Auxiliary accounts</td>
<td>3 times daily</td>
<td>Within 1 hour</td>
<td>Regular optimizer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Test accounts</td>
<td>Once daily</td>
<td>Within 4 hours</td>
<td>Junior or automation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Step 3: Set Tiered Alert Rules</h3>
<p>Main accounts get stricter alert thresholds:</p>
<ul>
<li>Main accounts: Alert if budget pace falls below 80%</li>
<li>Test accounts: Alert only if budget pace falls below 50%</li>
<li>High-risk accounts: Monitor only account survival status, not spend pace</li>
</ul>
<h2>Results: Changes After Three Months</h2>
<p>After implementing account tiering, three significant changes occurred:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Main account CPA dropped 12%</strong>: Optimizers had bandwidth for precise bid adjustments</li>
<li><strong>Ban-related losses reduced 60%</strong>: High-risk accounts handled in isolation, no longer affecting the business manager</li>
<li><strong>Team productivity increased 40%</strong>: Eliminated 80% of non-essential monitoring time<br />
n<br />
Zhou put it bluntly at the retrospective: "Before, we were managing accounts. Now, we're using accounts."</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p>Multi-account advertising isn't about "more is better"—it's about "more organized is better." If you're managing 5+ ad accounts, ask yourself: <strong>Is the time you spend on high-value accounts today proportional to the time spent on test accounts?</strong></p>
<p>If yes, your tiering is working. If not, it's time to adjust.</p>
